C('9-022',[41,42,43],'J156-9-06','6-46','Σ supports; C anti-involution; S⊥S′ ⇒ S∩S′=∅, non inverse','For symmetric irreflexive interior disjointness on an atelier A, double-perpendicular closed subsets form a complete ortholattice ΣA; meets are intersections, joins are closure of unions, and C is perpendicular. For supports S,T, S⊥T iff S⊆CT; this implies S∩T=∅, whose converse may fail. Constructible supports need not form a sublattice or be closed under C. X°={X}⊥⊥ is the least support containing X. Verification of the additional local support axiom is explicitly deferred.','proof sketched','ortholattice')
claims[-1]['lines'].append('J156-9-05:160-169')
C('9-023',[44,45,46,47],'J156-9-06','51-132','DépX ... Raff ... atelier spatial','For each stratum X, its deployment Dép(X) is the set of supports Y° for faces Y≤X, equipped with the transported FACE ORDER. These nonzero supports are pairwise orthogonal, so that order is not their ambient support-inclusion order. Refinement X≪Y induces a unique map of deployments assigning to each support a containing support, monotone for face orders; open refinement additionally sends the top face to the top face. An atelier is called spatial when the necessary deployment-refinement condition is also sufficient, so refinement is reconstructible from incidence and disjointness. The initial formula Raff must be read with the accompanying monotonicity requirement, explicitly included on scan54.','proof sketched','ateliers')
C('9-024',[47,48,49,50],'J156-9-06','134-226','structure d’atelier spatial ... seuls axiomes ... Dép injective','He reconstructs a spatial atelier from an incidence poset and a symmetric irreflexive relation satisfying face disjointness, provided X↦Dép(X) is injective; define refinement by support containment through monotone maps of deployments. The text checks the carrier and disjointness axioms. Its final summary Atspat2 on scan50 lists only X<Y⇒X⊥Y, weaker than pairwise disjointness of all distinct faces of one stratum used to form the deployments. This mismatch is retained as an axiom-reading issue; the reconstruction with the full face-disjointness premise is the precise supported formulation.','proof sketched','ateliers')
claims[-1]['lines'].append('J156-9-07:5-35')
C('9-025',[50,51,52,53],'J156-9-07','39-106','spatios (Σ,≤,C); ensemble des supports s’identifie à Σ','A spatios is a complete ortholattice: a complete bounded poset with an order-reversing involution C satisfying x∧Cx=0. On nonzero elements define x⊥y iff x≤Cy. Conversely every complete ortholattice is isomorphic to the double-perpendicular closed subsets of this orthogonality space, via x↦{y≠0:y≤x}; for a subset B its support corresponds to ∨B and its cosupport to C∨B. The variant allowing self-disjoint elements requires each such element be disjoint from every element; the resulting bottom is the universal-disjoint subset rather than necessarily ∅.','proved there','ortholattice')
C('9-026',[53,54,55,56],'J156-9-07','110-201','AtSpat(Σ) ... je présume ... application identique','For a complete ortholattice Σ, a spatial figure is a pair (Φ,ρ) with Φ a set of nonzero pairwise orthogonal elements and ρ an independent partial order; elementary means ρ has a greatest element. AtSpat(Σ) is the set of elementary spatial figures, with face restriction, refinement via unique support-containment maps monotone for ρ and preserving greatest elements, and disjointness of the greatest supports. He checks the atelier axioms and identifies its support ortholattice with Σ. The initially presumed spatiality is justified on scan56 by showing the canonical deployment map becomes the identity. The singleton section must be defined on Σ∖{0}, not on all Σ as printed.','corrected by himself later (both places)','ateliers')
C('9-027',[56,57],'J156-9-07','203-232','A→Atspat(ΣA) hom fidèle; spatial ... injectif sous-atelier','Every atelier has a canonical deployment map A→AtSpat(ΣA) preserving the structure and reflecting interior disjointness (called a faithful hom here). Spatiality means this map is injective and identifies A with the induced subatelier, whose image is face-closed. This “faithful hom” assertion concerns disjointness reflection and should not be conflated with the separately named class of faithful ateliers. A density condition on supports is still needed to characterize possible images.','stated without proof','ateliers')
C('9-028',[58,59,60,61],'J156-9-08','6-107','Atspat1′ ... Atspat2′ ... On a gagné!','Spatial ateliers are represented by pairs (Σ,A), with Σ a complete ortholattice and A a face-closed subset of AtSpat(Σ), such that the greatest supports α(X), X∈A, are join-dense: every S∈Σ equals ∨{α(X):α(X)≤S}. Then β(S)={X:α(X)≤S} identifies Σ with ΣA and transports the deployment map to the inclusion of A. The proof initially invokes a later general functoriality sorite whose unrestricted form subsequently fails; however the stronger join-density assumptions here allow a direct polarity proof, supplied separately in our audit. This theorem is a structural representation, not the previously absent pro-object reconstruction of c1/c2.','proved there','ateliers')
C('9-029',[61,62],'J156-9-08','109-161','disjoncteur ... correspondance ... catégorie','A disjoncteur is a set with symmetric disjointness (possibly self-disjoint elements only when universally disjoint). A morphism is a correspondence α:E→P(E′) taking every disjoint pair to pointwise-disjoint image sets. Relational composition and identity define a category. For ANY correspondence define α*(S)=cl_E′(⋃_{x∈S}α(x)) on double-polar closed subsets; existence of this monotone map alone gives no preservation of complement or composition.','definition/construction','orthomorphisms')
C('9-030',[63,64,65],'J156-9-08','168-283','formule essentielle (*) ... ça doit être faux ... contre-exemple immédiat','The attempted implication from mere disjointness preservation to α*(cl_E A)=cl_E′(α[A]) is not established; its proof is crossed out. The stronger proposed formula α*(B⊥)=(α[B])⊥ would imply it, but only the inclusion α*(B⊥)⊆(α[B])⊥ is proved. On scan65 Grothendieck disproves complement preservation using a nonsurjective injection between inequality-disjoint sets (continuation on scan66). That example refutes the stronger formula, not the first one, since all subsets are already closed. Our separate finite counterexample refutes the first formula itself under the stated hypotheses. These two failures must be distinguished.','corrected by himself later (both places)','orthomorphisms')
P('9-06','Local support axiom in general ateliers',[{'cote':'156-9','scan':43,'file':'J156-9-06','lines':'40'},{'cote':'156-9','scan':53,'file':'J156-9-07','lines':'129'}],'À vérifier encore cet axiome; stronger spatial-figure requirement set aside.','Identify which finite/allowed figures induce Boolean subalgebras of ΣA, and formulate compatibility with refinement. The complete ortholattice construction alone does not guarantee distributivity.')
P('9-07','Characterize spatial structures from incidence and disjointness',[{'cote':'156-9','scan':47,'file':'J156-9-06','lines':'134-146'},{'cote':'156-9','scan':58,'file':'J156-9-08','lines':'6-41'}],'When does (A,incidence,disjointness) come from a spatial atelier? Are face closure and support join-density sufficient?','The question is answered within scans48–60 under full pairwise face-disjointness and deployment injectivity, or via face-closed join-dense A⊆AtSpat(Σ). The final summary weakens an axiom and needs textual clarification; this is not an automatically still-open external problem.')
P('9-08','Functoriality of orthogonality support completion',[{'cote':'156-9','scan':63,'file':'J156-9-08','lines':'168-194'},{'cote':'156-9','scan':65,'file':'J156-9-08','lines':'263-283'}],'Disjointness compatibility does not prove the essential support/complement formulas; correction needed.','Determine exact additional hypotheses on correspondences ensuring preservation of double-polar closure, joins, orthocomplement, and composition. Treat these as separate properties. Continue with IX09 before judging the author’s final resolution.')
R('156-9',44,'J156-9-06:59','DépX has greatest element X°','greatest for transported face order, not Σ order','Its distinct elements are pairwise orthogonal nonzero supports and therefore incomparable under Σ inclusion; two orders are in use.')
R('156-9',46,'J156-9-06:103-125','Raff only ∀S∃T S≤T','also require monotonicity for deployment orders','Immediately following lines require monotonicity and scan54 definition includes it explicitly. Setwise containment alone does not encode refinement of ordered figures.')
R('156-9',50,'J156-9-07:15','Atspat2: X<Y⇒X⊥Y','distinct faces of a common stratum are pairwise orthogonal','The weaker comparable-pair axiom does not immediately supply the pairwise orthogonality asserted for deployments; same weakening appears in VIII scan31. No silent strengthening adopted.')
R('156-9',55,'J156-9-07:173-183','Σ→AtSpat(Σ), x↦({x},ρx)','Σ∖{0}→AtSpat(Σ)','Figspat2 forbids zero as a stratum support. Nonzero-element section suffices to reconstruct Σ, including its bottom.')
R('156-9',61,'J156-9-08:109-115','anti-réflexive, i.e. self-disjoint⇒universally disjoint','weaker null-element condition; not strict irreflexivity','The displayed condition allows self-disjoint null elements, whereas strict irreflexivity allows none. The previous spatios construction already distinguishes both cases.')
